*               gutter toggles the line's mark; the marks follow the text when a key or a click changes the number of lines (bp.follow).  Opt in: SCREEN MEMO never sets it.
* 2 Nov 26 dm  S.88 p3.3a: the op 'size' (a new width and height for the area, everything it holds kept), for $sl_widget_move; the rectangle is worked out in one
*              place (geom), read at every call as before and again by 'size'.
* 8 Nov 26 dm  S.179: Alt-K cuts the line as Ctrl-K does, for a program that keeps Ctrl-K for itself (the IDE: the keys list on every page).
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.184 stage 2 (SED retired into the IDE): SED's editing commands in the area - Alt-Backspace and Alt-D delete a word, Alt-E cuts to the end of the
*              line, Alt-T swaps two characters, Alt-U / Alt-L / Alt-C the word's case, Alt-Space closes a run of blanks, Alt-A aligns with the line above, Alt-M
*              swaps the cursor and the selection's other end, Ctrl-Up / Ctrl-Down nudge the view (sed.edit, sed.move).
* 09 Nov 26 dm S.184 stage 7: for long records, syntax colour keeps a checkpoint every 100 lines instead of running through every line above the top on each paint, and undo
*              holds 8,000,000 characters (it held 1,000,000: one step above about 500 KB).
* 09 Nov 26 dm S.184 stage 5: keyboard macros, SED's: Alt-R starts and ends the recording of the keys the area acts on, Alt-P plays them (macro.key).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_memo(op, w, scr, key, ret)      the widget contract; w is a collection (see $sl_label)
*
*   Elements:  id, ax ay (the top left cell of the area on the screen), w h (its width, the last column of
*   which is the scroll bar, and the number of rows it shows), value (the text: one line to a field, so a
*   dynamic array of lines), readonly (1: a viewer), wrap (1: long lines are wrapped at a blank), and the
*   state: cy and cx (the cursor's line and its place in it, 1 = before the first character), top (the first
*   ROW shown: a row is a line, or with wrap a part of one), left (the first column shown; 1 with wrap), want
*   (the column in its row the cursor tries to keep when it moves up or down), mx and my (the mark: the
*   other end of the selection, 0 when there is none), find (the text last searched for), undo and redo
*   (what Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-Y go back to), lastop (what the last key did, so that a run of typing is one undo), hbar
*   (1: a horizontal scroll bar in the last row of the area, drawn by $sl_hscrollbar, and one row less of text; none
*   when the text wraps; a click on its arrows scrolls 8 columns and on its track a page; set by the program, not by SCREEN MEMO), and
*   passkeys (key names, as values, that the area leaves alone and answers 'unhandled': the program uses them itself), and
*   softcur (1: while the area has the focus the character under the cursor is drawn in the highlight style, as well as the terminal's
*   own cursor being put there; not in a readonly area; set by the program, not by SCREEN MEMO), and
*   stay (1: Up on the first row and Down on the last do nothing instead of answering 'unhandled', so the form does not move the focus; set by the program), and
*   edkeys (1, the IDE's, 29 Oct 26: Ctrl-Home and Ctrl-End go to the start and the end of the text, Ctrl-Left and Ctrl-Right a word back and on, Ctrl-Del deletes the word at the
*   cursor (or the selection; at the end of a line it joins the next one) as one undo step 'delword'; without it these keys are 'unhandled'; set by the program, not by SCREEN MEMO), and
*   tabindent (a width, the IDE's 3: Tab and Shift-Tab indent and outdent - below - instead of answering 'unhandled'; not in a readonly area; set by the program,
*   not by SCREEN MEMO, so a user program's form keeps Tab for moving between its controls), and
*   bpon (1, the IDE's, 2 Nov 26: a mark cell in front of the gutter, one cell wider; a line in w{'bp'} (line numbers, ascending, field marks between them) shows a * in the hotkey style
*   there; a click in the gutter toggles the line; w{'bpmax'}, when above 0, is the most marks there can be; the marks move with the text when a key or a click changes the number
*   of lines; set by the program, not by SCREEN MEMO; needs 12 columns), and the ops
*     bptoggle  key<1> a line (0: the cursor's); ret is set, cleared, full (bpmax marks already) or none (not a line, or no bpon)
*     bpclear   takes every mark away
*     bpfollow  key is the text the marks were made for (lines with field marks between them): they are moved to the text now in w{'value'}; nothing is drawn, scr may be ''
*
*     init    w and h from what was given (at least 3 by 1); a linefeed in the text is taken as the end of a
*             line; the cursor goes to the start; no selection and nothing to undo
*     draw    the rows, from the first column shown, cut to the width and in the 'text' style (the selected
*             characters in the 'highlight' style), then the scroll bar in the last column (always there, so
*             the text does not move when it is needed)
*     focus   key = 1 gives it the focus (draws, with the cursor in the text), key = 0 takes it away
*     key     left, right (past the end of a line to the next, and back), up, down (a row at a time: with wrap
*             a line is several rows), home and end / ctrl-e (the row's ends), ctrl-p and ctrl-u
*             (a word back and on; ctrl-b and ctrl-f were these until 14 Oct 26, and are SED's find keys now), pgup and pgdn (scroll a page less one row and the cursor keeps its row on
*             the screen) move; a printable character, Return (splits the line), bs, del (joins lines at the ends),
*             ctrl-d (duplicates the line) and ctrl-k (cuts the whole line into the clipboard) edit.
*
*             The selection runs from the mark to the cursor.  ctrl-a selects all; ctrl-g sets the mark where the cursor is (and
*             takes it away again); ctrl-x cuts the selection, ctrl-c copies it, ctrl-v puts what was
*             cut or copied in at the cursor (the clipboard is the library's, so it passes from one text area
*             to another); typing, Return, bs and del on a selection replace or delete it.  ctrl-z undoes and
*             ctrl-y does again (a run of typed characters, or of bs or del, is one step; there are 100 steps).
*             ctrl-f (or ctrl-t) asks for text on the bottom row of the area and finds it, ctrl-b asks and finds
*             it backwards from the cursor, f3 finds the next one forwards (and a Return on the question,
*             which starts with the last text, repeats a find); the
*             search ignores case, goes on past the end of the text to its start, and selects what it found
*             (a bell if there is none).  A viewer can select, copy and find, and nothing else.
*
*             With tabindent set (28 Oct 26): tab puts blanks in at the cursor to the next stop (a multiple of the width, counted from the
*             first column), or with a selection puts the width's blanks before each selected line that holds something (a selection that ends
*             at the start of a line leaves that line out); btab takes out the blanks before the cursor's line, or before each selected line,
*             back to the stop before (at most the width, never anything but blanks).  Never a tab character; each press is one undo step, and a
*             btab that has nothing to take out changes nothing and makes no step.  The selection stays.
*
*             ret<1> is 'handled'; 'cancel' (Esc); or 'unhandled' with ret<2> the key's name: tab, btab, a
*             function key, and up on the first row and down on the last, so that a form can go on to the
*             widget before or after.  A readonly area moves: Return and every character are 'unhandled'.
*     mouse   a click puts the cursor on the character under it, the wheel scrolls three rows
*     size    key<1> a new width, key<2> a new height ('' keeps one; 2 Nov 26, for $sl_widget_move): the text, the cursor, the selection, the find text and the undo
*             and redo steps are kept, the view follows the cursor; nothing is drawn, scr may be ''
*     snap    the text and the cursor as they are go on the undo steps as a step of their own, and redo is forgotten; for a program that is
*             about to change the value itself (28 Oct 26).  The steps are kept as they are: lines holding value, subvalue or text marks come back from an undo whole.
*     close   nothing to undo: the caller repaints what is under it
*
*   Wrapping breaks a line after the last blank that fits, or in the middle of a word that is longer than
*   the width; the blank stays at the end of its row, and the line is not changed.  Without wrap a longer
*   line is shown from the first column shown and the area pans sideways to follow the cursor.  A tab
*   character is shown as a blank.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_memo(op, w, scr, key, ret)
$catalog $sl_memo

$include sl.h

   common /slclip/ clip.has, clip.text

   ret = ''
   pr.kind = ''                                  ;* paint.plan: every paint draws the whole area unless macro.key says otherwise
   ro = w{'readonly'} + 0
   ti = w{'tabindent'} + 0                       ;* 28 Oct 26: the indent width Tab and Shift-Tab use (0: they are not the area's keys)
   if ti < 0 then ti = 0
   ek = w{'edkeys'} + 0                          ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): the editor's keys Ctrl-Home, Ctrl-End, Ctrl-Left, Ctrl-Right and Ctrl-Del (an opt-in of the program; 0: they are 'unhandled')
   ov = w{'over'} + 0                            ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): overwrite mode (with edkeys, Ins toggles it; the program reads w{'over'} to show it): a typed character replaces the one under the cursor
   ai = w{'autoindent'} + 0                      ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): Return copies the blanks the line begins with (up to the cursor) onto the new line; set by the program
   wrap = w{'wrap'} + 0
   lines = w{'value'}
   lnum = w{'lnum'} + 0                          ;* 13 Oct 26: line numbers down the left (gut cells: the digits and a blank)
   syl = w{'synlang'} + 0                        ;* 13 Oct 26: the number of the language the text is coloured as (0: none)
   bpl = w{'bp'}
   ob0 = lines                                   ;* the text the marks are kept for: store moves them when the number of lines is not the same as at the end of this call
   gosub geom
   cx = w{'cx'} + 0
   cy = w{'cy'} + 0
   top = w{'top'} + 0
   left = w{'left'} + 0
   want = w{'want'} + 0
   mx = w{'mx'} + 0
   my = w{'my'} + 0
   fnd = w{'find'}
   * 28 Oct 26 (S.88 phase 2, D3): the undo and redo steps are an entry each in the widget (see snap.push), not one string cut at marks: here only the counts are read
   uk.b = w{'ukb'} + 0 ; uk.n = w{'ukn'} + 0 ; uk.t = w{'ukt'} + 0
   rk.b = w{'rkb'} + 0 ; rk.n = w{'rkn'} + 0 ; rk.t = w{'rkt'} + 0
   if w{'undo'} # '' then gosub snap.legacy
   if wrap then left = 1

   begin case
      case op = 'init'
         w{'type'} = 'memo'
         w{'focusable'} = 1
         w{'w'} = mw
         w{'h'} = mh + hb
         lines = convert(char(10), @fm, lines)
         cx = 1 ; cy = 1 ; top = 1 ; left = 1 ; want = 1
         mx = 0 ; my = 0
         gosub snap.clear                             ;* nothing to undo or redo
         w{'lastop'} = ''
         w{'maxn'} = ''                               ;* the longest line is measured again (get.maxl)
         gosub store

      case op = 'snap'
         * 28 Oct 26: the text and the cursor as they are now go on the undo steps as one step of their own and redo is forgotten (a program that is about to change w{'value'} itself, as the IDE does for an AI edit)
         kind = 'snap' ; k0 = ''
         gosub snap.push
         gosub store

      case op = 'draw'
         gosub count
         gosub paint

      case op = 'focus'
         w{'focused'} = if key then 1 else 0
         gosub count
         gosub paint

      case op = 'key'
         gosub count
         gosub macro.key

      case op = 'mouse'
         gosub count
         gosub click

      case op = 'size'
         * 2 Nov 26 (S.88 p3.3a): key<1> the new width, key<2> the new height ('' keeps one), from $sl_widget_move.  The text, the cursor, the selection, the find
         * text and the undo and redo steps stay as they are; the view follows the cursor into the new rectangle.  Nothing is drawn and init is not run.
         if key<1> # '' then w{'w'} = key<1> + 0
         if key<2> # '' then w{'h'} = key<2> + 0
         gosub geom
         w{'w'} = mw
         w{'h'} = mh + hb
         gosub count
         gosub scroll
         gosub store
         ret = 'handled'

      case op = 'bptoggle'
         gosub count
         bl = key<1> + 0
         if bl = 0 then bl = cy
         gosub bp.toggle
         if ret # 'none' and scr # '' then gosub paint       ;* a program that draws the widget itself (the IDE) gives scr as ''

      case op = 'bpclear'
         ret = 'none'
         if bpon then
            w{'bp'} = ''
            ret = 'cleared'
            gosub count
            if scr # '' then gosub paint
         end

      case op = 'bpfollow'
         ob0 = key                                 ;* the text the marks were made for
         gosub bp.follow
   end case

   return

* The area's rectangle from w{'w'} and w{'h'} (2 Nov 26: one place, read at every call and again by 'size'): mw mh (the text's rows), hb (the horizontal bar), gut (the
* gutter: the line numbers gn and the mark cell of bpon) and tw (the width of the text).  In: wrap, lnum, lines.
geom:
   mw = w{'w'} + 0
   mh = w{'h'} + 0
   if mw < 3 then mw = 3
   if mh < 1 then mh = 1
   * 18 Oct 26: hbar (1) puts a horizontal scroll bar in the last row of the area, and the text has one row less.  Opt in: a program that
   * does not set it (SCREEN MEMO) is drawn as it always was.  There is none when the text wraps (nothing to scroll sideways) or the area is
   * under three rows high.
   hb = 0
   if w{'hbar'} + 0 and not(wrap) and mh >= 3 then
      hb = 1
      mh -= 1
   end
   gut = 0
   if lnum then
      gosub line.count
      gut = len(max(lc, 1)) + 1
      if gut > mw - 4 then gut = 0               ;* an area too narrow for them has none
   end
   gn = gut                                      ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the width of the line numbers; the mark cell of bpon is in front of them
   bpon = w{'bpon'} + 0
   if mw < 12 then bpon = 0
   if bpon then gut += 1
   tw = mw - 1 - gut                            ;* the width of the text: the last column is the scroll bar
   return

* A click puts the cursor on the character under it (after the last one of a shorter row, on the last row if it
* is below the text); the wheel scrolls three rows and the cursor goes with it if it would leave the screen
click:
   ret = 'handled'
   w{'lastop'} = ''
   if hb then
      if key<1> # 'wheel' and key<4> = w{'ay'} + mh then
         gosub hbar.click
         gosub count
         gosub scroll
         gosub store
         gosub paint
         return
      end
   end
   if bpon then
      if key<1> # 'wheel' and key<3> - w{'ax'} < gut then
         * 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): a click in the gutter toggles the mark of the line clicked (the first row of a wrapped line is the line's; the cursor, the selection and the view stay)
         rr = top + key<4> - w{'ay'}
         if rr > nr then rr = nr
         if rr < 1 then rr = 1
         gosub row.text
         bl = rline
         gosub bp.toggle
         gosub paint
         return
      end
   end
   if key<1> = 'wheel' then
      if key<2> = 'up' then top -= 3 else top += 3
      if top > nr - mh + 1 then top = nr - mh + 1
      if top < 1 then top = 1
      gosub row.of
      rr = dr
      if rr < top then rr = top
      if rr > top + mh - 1 then rr = top + mh - 1
      if rr > nr then rr = nr
      gosub to.row
   end else
      rr = top + key<4> - w{'ay'}
      if rr > nr then rr = nr
      if rr < 1 then rr = 1
      want = left + key<3> - w{'ax'} - gut
      if want < 1 then want = 1
      gosub to.row
      gosub row.of
      want = dcx
   end
   gosub count
   gosub scroll
   gosub store
   gosub paint
   return

* A click on the horizontal bar (hb): the left arrow scrolls 8 columns back, the right one 8 on, the track before the thumb a page back and
* after it a page on; the thumb itself does nothing.  The cursor goes with the view if it would leave it, as it does for the wheel.
hbar.click:
   c = key<3> - w{'ax'} - gut                    ;* the cell of the bar, from 0
   if c < 0 or c >= tw then return
   gosub get.maxl
   call $sl_hscrollbar(tw, left, maxl, tw, hbar)
   call $sl_line('thumb', '', hth)
   begin case
      case c = 0
         left -= 8
      case c = tw - 1
         left += 8
      case hbar[c + 1, 1] = hth
         null
      case c + 1 < index(hbar, hth, 1)
         left -= tw - 1
      case 1
         left += tw - 1
   end case
   if left > maxl - tw + 1 then left = maxl - tw + 1
   if left < 1 then left = 1
   gosub row.of
   rr = dr
   want = dcx
   if want < left then want = left
   if want > left + tw - 1 then want = left + tw - 1
   gosub to.row
   return

* maxl = the length of the longest line, plus one: the width of the text the horizontal bar stands for.  Measuring it is O(the text), and a
* bar that measured it at every paint made every key in a 30,000-line record cost 85 ms (the verifier: 3.5 s for 40 characters, 0.19 s without
* the bar).  So it is kept in the widget (maxl, with the length of the whole text it was measured at, maxn) and measured again only when the
* text has changed length by more than 200 characters since (a typed character moves it by one) or the line the cursor is on is longer than
* it was.  After a short line is deleted the bar can be a few columns out until the next measure; that is all it costs.
get.maxl:
   tl = len(lines)
   mn = w{'maxn'}
   if mn = '' or abs(tl - mn) > 200 then
      maxl = maximum(lens(lines)) + 1
      w{'maxl'} = maxl
      w{'maxn'} = tl
   end else
      maxl = w{'maxl'} + 0
      cl = len(lines<cy>) + 1
      if cl > maxl then
         maxl = cl
         w{'maxl'} = maxl
      end
   end
   return

* n = the number of lines, nr the number of rows (the same without wrap); a text with nothing in it has one
count:
   gosub line.count
   n = lc
   if n < 1 then n = 1
   if cy < 1 then cy = 1
   if cy > n then cy = n
   if cx < 1 then cx = 1
   if top < 1 then top = 1
   if left < 1 then left = 1
   if wrap then
      left = 1
      gosub build.map
   end else
      nr = n
   end
   return

* lc = the number of lines.  Counting them runs over the whole text, three times a key, so the count is kept with the text it was made from (9 Nov 26, S.184 stage 7; the length alone
*   would not do: a program can put in a text of the same length with other lines).  Comparing is far cheaper than counting.  A typed character (macro.key) keeps the count.
line.count:
   lc.t = w{'nct'}
   if len(lc.t) > 0 and lc.t = lines then
      lc = w{'nc'} + 0
   end else
      lc = dcount(lines, @fm)
      w{'nc'} = lc
      w{'nct'} = lines
   end
   return

* The rows of a wrapped text.  Row r is part of line rl(r), from the character rs(r) on, rn(r) characters; fr(i) is
* the first row of line i.  A row but the last of its line is cut after the last blank that fits in tw characters, or
* at tw characters if it holds none; the last row of a line needs a place for the cursor after its last character,
* so it is at most tw - 1 long.
build.map:
   tot = 0
   for i = 1 to n
      tot += len(lines<i>)
   next i
   bound = (n * 2) + (int(tot / tw) * 4) + 8
   dim rl(bound + 1)
   dim rs(bound + 1)
   dim rn(bound + 1)
   dim fr(n + 1)
   r = 0
   for i = 1 to n
      t = lines<i>
      tlen = len(t)
      s = 1
      fr(i) = r + 1
      done = @false
      loop
         r += 1
         rl(r) = i
         rs(r) = s
         rlen.left = tlen - s + 1
         if rlen.left <= tw - 1 or r >= bound then
            rn(r) = rlen.left
            done = @true
         end else
            seg = t[s, tw]
            p = tw
            loop
            while p > 0 and seg[p, 1] # ' '
               p -= 1
            repeat
            if p = 0 then p = tw
            rn(r) = p
            s += p
         end
      until done
      repeat
   next i
   nr = r
   rl(nr + 1) = 0                            ;* the row after the last: every part of a test on it is assigned
   rs(nr + 1) = 0
   rn(nr + 1) = 0
   return

* rr (a row) -> rline (its line), rstart (its first character in the line), rtext (what it holds), rlen (its length),
* rlast (it is the last row of its line), rcm (the highest column the cursor can have in it)
row.text:
   if wrap then
      rline = rl(rr)
      rstart = rs(rr)
      rlen = rn(rr)
      rtext = lines<rline>[rstart, rlen]
      rlast = (rl(rr + 1) # rline)
   end else
      rline = rr
      rstart = 1
      rtext = lines<rr>
      rlen = len(rtext)
      rlast = @true
   end
   rcm = rlen
   if rlast then rcm = rlen + 1
   return

* The cursor (cy, cx) -> dr (the row it is on) and dcx (its column in the row, 1 = the row's first character)
row.of:
   if wrap then
      dr = fr(cy)
      loop
      while dr < nr and rl(dr + 1) = cy and rs(dr + 1) <= cx
         dr += 1
      repeat
      dcx = cx - rs(dr) + 1
   end else
      dr = cy
      dcx = cx
   end
   return

* Put the cursor on row rr, in the column want (or the row's last place if it is shorter)
to.row:
   gosub row.text
   cy = rline
   dc = want
   if dc > rcm then dc = rcm
   if dc < 1 then dc = 1
   cx = rstart + dc - 1
   return

* The marks (bpon).  bp.toggle: In: bl (a line), bpl; Out: ret (set, cleared, full, none) and w{'bp'}.  The marks are line numbers, ascending, one to a field.
bp.toggle:
   ret = 'none'
   if bpon then
      nl = dcount(lines, @fm)
      if nl < 1 then nl = 1
      if bl >= 1 and bl <= nl then
         bp.n = dcount(bpl, @fm)
         if bpl = '' then bp.n = 0
         bp.at = 0
         for bi = 1 to bp.n
            if bpl<bi> + 0 >= bl then
               bp.at = bi
               exit
            end
         next bi
         bp.max = w{'bpmax'} + 0
         begin case
            case bp.at > 0 and bpl<bp.at> + 0 = bl
               del bpl<bp.at>
               ret = 'cleared'
            case bp.max > 0 and bp.n >= bp.max
               ret = 'full'
            case bp.at = 0
               bpl<-1> = bl
               ret = 'set'
            case 1
               ins bl before bpl<bp.at>
               ret = 'set'
         end case
         w{'bp'} = bpl
      end
   end
   return

* bp.follow: In: ob0 (the text the marks were made for), lines (the text now), bpl.  When the number of lines is not the same, the lines the two texts share at the start (bp.p) and at the end
* (bp.s) are found: a mark in the first part stays, one in the last part moves by the difference, one in what was replaced stays only if it is still inside what replaced it.
bp.follow:
   if bpl # '' then
      bp.oc = dcount(ob0, @fm)
      bp.nc = dcount(lines, @fm)
      if bp.oc < 1 then bp.oc = 1
      if bp.nc < 1 then bp.nc = 1
      if bp.oc # bp.nc then
         bp.lo = bp.oc
         if bp.nc < bp.lo then bp.lo = bp.nc
         bp.p = 0
         for bi = 1 to bp.lo
            if ob0<bi> # lines<bi> then exit
            bp.p = bi
         next bi
         bp.s = 0
         for bi = 1 to bp.lo - bp.p
            bp.ox = bp.oc - bi + 1
            bp.nx = bp.nc - bi + 1
            if ob0<bp.ox> # lines<bp.nx> then exit
            bp.s = bi
         next bi
         bp.new = ''
         for bi = 1 to dcount(bpl, @fm)
            bp.m = bpl<bi> + 0
            begin case
               case bp.m <= bp.p
               case bp.m > bp.oc - bp.s
                  bp.m += bp.nc - bp.oc
               case bp.m > bp.nc - bp.s
                  bp.m = 0
            end case
            if bp.m >= 1 and bp.m <= bp.nc then bp.new<-1> = bp.m
         next bi
         bpl = bp.new
         w{'bp'} = bpl
      end
   end
   return

store:
   if bpl # '' then
      if lines # ob0 then
         gosub bp.follow
         ob0 = lines
      end
   end
   w{'value'} = lines
   w{'cx'} = cx
   w{'cy'} = cy
   w{'top'} = top
   w{'left'} = left
   w{'want'} = want
   w{'mx'} = mx
   w{'my'} = my
   w{'find'} = fnd
   w{'ukb'} = uk.b ; w{'ukn'} = uk.n ; w{'ukt'} = uk.t
   w{'rkb'} = rk.b ; w{'rkn'} = rk.n ; w{'rkt'} = rk.t
   return

* Keep the cursor inside the rows and columns shown
scroll:
   gosub row.of
   if dr < top then top = dr
   if dr > top + mh - 1 then top = dr - mh + 1
   if wrap then
      left = 1
   end else
      if cx < left then left = cx
      if cx > left + tw - 1 then left = cx - tw + 1
   end
   if top < 1 then top = 1
   if left < 1 then left = 1
   return

* ======================================================================
* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 2) - SED's editing commands (the owner, 9 Nov: SED and CED are retired once the IDE has all their features).  One undo step each.
*   alt-bs  delete the word before the cursor (the blanks before it and the word), or the selection; at the start of a line join it to the line above
*   alt-d   delete the word at the cursor, as Ctrl-Del does in an area with edkeys
*   alt-e   cut from the cursor to the end of the line into the clipboard; at the end of a line join the next line to it
*   alt-t   swap the character before the cursor with the one at it (at the end of a line, the last two), and move on
*   alt-u, alt-l, alt-c   the word at or after the cursor in capitals, in small letters, or with a capital initial; the cursor goes to its end
*   alt-    (Alt and the space bar) a run of blanks at the cursor becomes one blank
*   alt-a   the line is indented as the line above it is
* A key that has nothing to act on (no word, one character) rings the bell and changes nothing.

sed.edit:
   ln = lines<cy>
   begin case
      case key = 'alt-bs'
         kind = 'delword'
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then
            gosub del.sel
         end else
            if cx > 1 then
               cb = cx - 1
               loop
               while cb >= 1 and ln[cb, 1] = ' '
                  cb -= 1
               repeat
               loop
               while cb >= 1 and ln[cb, 1] # ' '
                  cb -= 1
               repeat
               lines<cy> = ln[1, cb] : ln[cx, len(ln)]
               cx = cb + 1
            end else
               if cy > 1 then
                  cx = len(lines<cy - 1>) + 1
                  lines<cy - 1> = lines<cy - 1> : ln
                  del lines<cy>
                  cy -= 1
               end
            end
         end
      case key = 'alt-d'
         kind = 'delword'
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then
            gosub del.sel
         end else
            if cx <= len(ln) then
               ce = cx
               loop
               while ce <= len(ln) and ln[ce, 1] = ' '
                  ce += 1
               repeat
               loop
               while ce <= len(ln) and ln[ce, 1] # ' '
                  ce += 1
               repeat
               lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : ln[ce, len(ln)]
            end else
               if cy < n then
                  lines<cy> = ln : lines<cy + 1>
                  del lines<cy + 1>
               end
            end
         end
      case key = 'alt-e'
         kind = 'killend'
         gosub snap.push
         if cx <= len(ln) then
            clip.text = ln[cx, len(ln)]
            clip.has = 1
            lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1]
         end else
            if cy < n then
               lines<cy> = ln : lines<cy + 1>
               del lines<cy + 1>
            end
         end
      case key = 'alt-t'
         if len(ln) < 2 then
            gosub ring.bell
         end else
            p = cx
            if p > len(ln) then p = len(ln)
            if p < 2 then p = 2
            kind = 'transpose'
            gosub snap.push
            lines<cy> = ln[1, p - 2] : ln[p, 1] : ln[p - 1, 1] : ln[p + 1, len(ln)]
            cx = p + 1
         end
      case key = 'alt-u' or key = 'alt-l' or key = 'alt-c'
         ws = cx
         loop
         while ws <= len(ln) and not(ln[ws, 1] matches '1A' or ln[ws, 1] matches '1N')
            ws += 1
         repeat
         we = ws
         loop
         while we <= len(ln) and (ln[we, 1] matches '1A' or ln[we, 1] matches '1N')
            we += 1
         repeat
         if we = ws then
            gosub ring.bell
         end else
            kind = 'wordcase'
            gosub snap.push
            wd = ln[ws, we - ws]
            begin case
               case key = 'alt-u'
                  wd = upcase(wd)
               case key = 'alt-l'
                  wd = downcase(wd)
               case 1
                  wd = upcase(wd[1, 1]) : downcase(wd[2, len(wd)])
            end case
            lines<cy> = ln[1, ws - 1] : wd : ln[we, len(ln)]
            cx = we
         end
      case key = 'alt- '
         cs = cx
         loop
         while cs > 1 and ln[cs - 1, 1] = ' '
            cs -= 1
         repeat
         ce = cx
         loop
         while ce <= len(ln) and ln[ce, 1] = ' '
            ce += 1
         repeat
         if ce - cs < 2 then
            gosub ring.bell              ;* no run of two blanks here
         end else
            kind = 'closesp'
            gosub snap.push
            lines<cy> = ln[1, cs - 1] : ' ' : ln[ce, len(ln)]
            cx = cs + 1
         end
      case key = 'alt-a'
         if cy < 2 then
            gosub ring.bell
         end else
            pl = lines<cy - 1>
            al.p = len(pl) - len(trimf(pl))
            if trim(pl) = '' then al.p = 0
            al.c = len(ln) - len(trimf(ln))
            if trim(ln) = '' then al.c = len(ln)
            kind = 'align'
            gosub snap.push
            lines<cy> = space(al.p) : ln[al.c + 1, len(ln)]
            cx += al.p - al.c
            if cx < al.p + 1 then cx = al.p + 1
         end
   end case
   if cx < 1 then cx = 1
   want = cx
   my = 0 ; mx = 0
   return

* alt-m swaps the cursor and the other end of the selection (SED's swap mark); ctrl-up / ctrl-down move the view a line up or down, the cursor staying on its line while
*   that line can be seen (SED's nudge).  Neither changes the text.
sed.move:
   begin case
      case key = 'alt-m'
         if my > 0 and my <= n then
            sw.y = my ; sw.x = mx
            my = cy ; mx = cx
            cy = sw.y ; cx = sw.x
            want = cx
         end else
            gosub ring.bell
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-down'
         if top + mh - 1 < n then
            top += 1
            if cy < top then cy = top
         end
         vert = @true
      case key = 'ctrl-up'
         if top > 1 then
            top -= 1
            if cy > top + mh - 1 then cy = top + mh - 1
         end
         vert = @true
   end case
   ln = lines<cy>
   if cx > len(ln) + 1 then cx = len(ln) + 1
   return

* The bell, for a key that has nothing to act on: the memo's one place that rings it
ring.bell:
   display char(7) :
   return

* has.sel = a selection exists: it runs from (sy, sx) to (ey, ex), the end not included
sel.range:
   has.sel = @false
   if my > 0 and my <= n then
      if my < cy or (my = cy and mx <= cx) then
         sy = my ; sx = mx ; ey = cy ; ex = cx
      end else
         sy = cy ; sx = cx ; ey = my ; ex = mx
      end
      if sy # ey or sx # ex then has.sel = @true
   end
   return

* st = the selected text, one line to a field
sel.text:
   if sy = ey then
      st = lines<sy>[sx, ex - sx]
   end else
      st = lines<sy>[sx, 999999]
      for i = sy + 1 to ey - 1
         st := @fm : lines<i>
      next i
      st := @fm : lines<ey>[1, ex - 1]
   end
   return

* Take the selection out; the cursor goes to where it began
del.sel:
   head = lines<sy>[1, sx - 1]
   tail = lines<ey>[ex, 999999]
   if ey - sy > 100 then
      * 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): a long selection is cut out once (the lines before, the joined line, the lines after); a DEL for each of thousands of lines is quadratic
      nn = dcount(lines, @fm)
      nl = ''
      if sy > 1 then nl = field(lines, @fm, 1, sy - 1) : @fm
      nl := head : tail
      if ey < nn then nl := @fm : field(lines, @fm, ey + 1, 99999999)
      lines = nl
   end else
      lines<sy> = head : tail
      for i = ey to sy + 1 step -1
         del lines<i>
      next i
   end
   cy = sy
   cx = sx
   want = cx
   my = 0 ; mx = 0
   return

* Before an edit, kind says what it is: remember the text so that it can be undone.  A run of typing, of bs or of del
* is one step (k0 is what the key before did).
* 28 Oct 26 (S.88 phase 2, D3): THE STEPS ARE KEPT AS THEY ARE.  Until then the whole stack was one string: a step's lines had their field marks turned into value marks and the stack was
*   cut at field marks and the step at text marks, so a record whose lines held a value, subvalue or text mark came back from an undo changed (a value mark became a line end, a text mark cut
*   the text short).  Now a step is a string of its own in the widget, w{'u' : n} for undo and w{'r' : n} for redo, n running from the base + 1 to the base + the count (the base moves up when
*   the oldest step is dropped): the cursor's line, a blank, the cursor's place, a blank, then the lines VERBATIM.  Nothing is searched for in the lines: the two blanks are found from the front.
*   The last step is the one to go back to.  The counts are uk.b uk.n uk.t (base, steps, characters) and rk.b rk.n rk.t for redo.
snap.push:
   if kind = k0 and (kind = 'type' or kind = 'bs' or kind = 'del') then return
   gosub snap.make
   gosub snap.add
   gosub snap.redo.clear
   return

* snap goes on the end of the undo steps; at most 100 steps and 8,000,000 characters (SED's limit, 9 Nov 26), the oldest going first (the newest is always kept)
snap.add:
   uk.n += 1
   uk.t += len(snap)
   w{'u' : (uk.b + uk.n)} = snap
   loop
   while uk.n > 100 or (uk.t > 8000000 and uk.n > 1)
      uk.t -= len(w{'u' : (uk.b + 1)})
      w{'u' : (uk.b + 1)} = ''
      uk.b += 1
      uk.n -= 1
   repeat
   return

* a new edit ends the redo list
snap.redo.clear:
   for sn.i = rk.b + 1 to rk.b + rk.n
      w{'r' : sn.i} = ''
   next sn.i
   rk.b = 0 ; rk.n = 0 ; rk.t = 0
   return

* A program that put a step on the key the memo used before 28 Oct 26 (the IDE did, for the change an AI edit makes) is still understood: the old form was fields cut at field marks, each
* the cursor's line, a text mark, its place, a text mark and the lines with value marks for the ends of lines (newest first).  They are made steps as they come; redo is forgotten, as the writer meant.
snap.legacy:
   sn.lg = w{'undo'}
   w{'undo'} = ''
   w{'redo'} = ''
   for sn.k = dcount(sn.lg, @fm) to 1 step -1
      sn.s = sn.lg<sn.k>
      if sn.s # '' then
         snap = field(sn.s, @tm, 1) : ' ' : field(sn.s, @tm, 2) : ' ' : convert(@vm, @fm, field(sn.s, @tm, 3, 99999))
         gosub snap.add
      end
   next sn.k
   gosub snap.redo.clear
   w{'ukb'} = uk.b ; w{'ukn'} = uk.n ; w{'ukt'} = uk.t
   w{'rkb'} = rk.b ; w{'rkn'} = rk.n ; w{'rkt'} = rk.t
   return

* snap = the text and the cursor as one string: line, blank, place, blank, the lines as they are
snap.make:
   snap = cy : ' ' : cx : ' ' : lines
   return

* Nothing to undo and nothing to redo: the steps still in the widget are blanked
snap.clear:
   for sn.i = uk.b + 1 to uk.b + uk.n
      w{'u' : sn.i} = ''
   next sn.i
   for sn.i = rk.b + 1 to rk.b + rk.n
      w{'r' : sn.i} = ''
   next sn.i
   uk.b = 0 ; uk.n = 0 ; uk.t = 0
   rk.b = 0 ; rk.n = 0 ; rk.t = 0
   return

* Ctrl-Z: back to the last undo step; what the text is now goes on the redo list
undo.step:
   if uk.n < 1 then return
   cur = w{'u' : (uk.b + uk.n)}
   if cur = '' then return
   w{'u' : (uk.b + uk.n)} = ''
   uk.t -= len(cur)
   uk.n -= 1
   gosub snap.make
   rk.n += 1
   rk.t += len(snap)
   w{'r' : (rk.b + rk.n)} = snap
   gosub snap.apply
   return

* Ctrl-Y: on again, the other way round
redo.step:
   if rk.n < 1 then return
   cur = w{'r' : (rk.b + rk.n)}
   if cur = '' then return
   w{'r' : (rk.b + rk.n)} = ''
   rk.t -= len(cur)
   rk.n -= 1
   gosub snap.make
   uk.n += 1
   uk.t += len(snap)
   w{'u' : (uk.b + uk.n)} = snap
   gosub snap.apply
   return

* cur = a step: the text and the cursor become what it holds
snap.apply:
   sn.p = index(cur, ' ', 2)                     ;* the second blank: what follows is the lines, as they were
   lines = cur[sn.p + 1, len(cur)]
   cy = field(cur, ' ', 1) + 0
   cx = field(cur, ' ', 2) + 0
   want = cx
   my = 0 ; mx = 0
   return

* Put the clipboard in at the cursor
paste:
   t = clip.text
paste.t:
   ln = lines<cy>
   nt = dcount(t, @fm)
   if nt <= 1 then
      lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : t : ln[cx, 999999]
      cx += len(t)
   end else
      head = ln[1, cx - 1]
      tail = ln[cx, 999999]
      if nt > 100 then
         * 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): a long paste is put together once (the lines before, the pasted lines, the lines after); an INS for each of thousands of lines is quadratic
         nn = dcount(lines, @fm)
         nl = ''
         if cy > 1 then nl = field(lines, @fm, 1, cy - 1) : @fm
         nl := head : t<1> : @fm : field(t, @fm, 2, nt - 2) : @fm : t<nt> : tail
         if cy < nn then nl := @fm : field(lines, @fm, cy + 1, 99999999)
         lines = nl
      end else
         lines<cy> = head : t<1>
         for i = 2 to nt - 1
            ins t<i> before lines<cy + i - 1>
         next i
         ins t<nt> : tail before lines<cy + nt - 1>
      end
      cy += nt - 1
      cx = len(t<nt>) + 1
   end
   want = cx
   return

* The next place the text in find is, from the cursor on (ignoring case); selected, or a bell
find.next:
   if fnd = '' then return
   fl = upcase(fnd)
   y = cy
   found = @false
   for pn = 0 to n
      t = upcase(lines<y>)
      st0 = 1
      if pn = 0 then st0 = cx
      p = index(t[st0, 999999], fl, 1)
      if p > 0 then
         found = @true
         exit
      end
      y += 1
      if y > n then y = 1
   next pn
   if found then
      my = y ; mx = st0 + p - 1
      cy = y ; cx = mx + len(fnd)
      want = cx
   end else
      gosub ring.bell
   end
   return

* Ask for the text on the bottom row of the area
find.prompt:
   pw = ''
   pw<SL.W.Y> = w{'ay'} + mh - 1
   pw<SL.W.D> = if fdir = -1 then 'Find backward: ' else 'Find: '
   pw<SL.W.D + 1> = fnd
   call $sl_prompt('init', pw, scr, '', pr)
   call $sl_prompt('run', pw, scr, '', pr)
   if pr = 'enter' then
      fnd = pw<SL.W.VALUE>
      if fdir = -1 then gosub find.prev else gosub find.next
   end
   return

* The same text again, backwards from the cursor (or from the start of the selection a find made): the last
* occurrence that ends before it, in the lines above, and on past the start of the text to its end
find.prev:
   if fnd = '' then return
   fl = upcase(fnd)
   y = cy
   found = @false
   p = 0
   for pn = 0 to n
      t = upcase(lines<y>)
      if pn = 0 then
         lim = cx - 1
         if my > 0 and my = cy and mx >= 1 and mx <= cx then lim = mx - 1
         seg = t[1, lim]
      end else
         seg = t
      end
      cnt = count(seg, fl)
      if cnt > 0 then
         p = index(seg, fl, cnt)
         found = @true
         exit
      end
      y -= 1
      if y < 1 then y = n
   next pn
   if found then
      my = y ; mx = p
      cy = y ; cx = p + len(fnd)
      want = cx
   end else
      gosub ring.bell
   end
   return

* A key, through the keyboard macro (9 Nov 26, S.184 stage 5).  Alt-R starts a recording (the old macro goes) and ends it; while it is on, every key the area acted on is
*   kept in w{'macro'}, as fields; a key it passes back (a function key, a menu) and a paste are not.  Alt-P plays the keys, each as if typed, and stops at one the area does not
*   act on; each is its own undo step, as it was when typed.  Not while recording, and neither key does anything while a macro plays.  A program's passkeys come first.
macro.key:
   if (key = 'alt-r' or key = 'alt-p') and not(index(@vm : w{'passkeys'} : @vm, @vm : key : @vm, 1)) then
      ret = 'handled'
      if w{'macrun'} + 0 then return
      if key = 'alt-r' then
         if w{'macrec'} + 0 then
            w{'macrec'} = 0
         end else
            w{'macrec'} = 1
            w{'macro'} = ''
         end
         return
      end
      if w{'macrec'} + 0 or w{'macro'} = '' then return
      w{'macrun'} = 1
      m.list = w{'macro'}
      m.n = dcount(m.list, @fm)
      for m.i = 1 to m.n
         call $sl_memo('key', w, scr, m.list<m.i>, m.ret)
      while m.ret<1> = 'handled'
      next m.i
      w{'macrun'} = 0
      return
   end
   m.key = key
   m.len = len(lines)
   m.cy = cy
   w{'synkey'} = (len(key) = 1 or key = 'bs' or key = 'del' or key = 'enter')      ;* these change the text at the cursor's line and nowhere above it (syn.sync)
   pr.kind = ''                                  ;* paint.plan: what this key can have changed on the screen
   if len(key) = 1 or key = 'bs' or key = 'del' then pr.kind = 'edit'
   if key = 'up' or key = 'down' or key = 'left' or key = 'right' or key = 'home' or key = 'end' then pr.kind = 'move'
   gosub handle.key
   if len(m.key) = 1 and len(lines) = m.len + 1 then w{'nct'} = lines              ;* a typed character: as many lines as before (line.count)
   if w{'macrec'} + 0 and ret<1> = 'handled' and m.key # 'paste' then
      m.list = w{'macro'}
      m.list<-1> = m.key
      w{'macro'} = m.list
   end
   return

handle.key:
   ret = 'handled'
   k0 = w{'lastop'}                 ;* what the key before did; a key that edits says what it did at the end
   w{'lastop'} = ''
   kind = ''
   vert = @false
   * 18 Oct 26: passkeys (values) are key names the program keeps for itself; the area does not use them (the IDE's Ctrl-E, which hands
   *   the record to SED, is the end-of-row key here).  Nothing is changed or drawn for one.
   if w{'passkeys'} # '' then
      if index(@vm : w{'passkeys'} : @vm, @vm : key : @vm, 1) then
         ret = 'unhandled' : @fm : key
         return
      end
   end
   gosub sel.range
   begin case
      case key = 'esc'
         ret = 'cancel'
      case key = 'up'
         gosub row.of
         if dr > 1 then
            rr = dr - 1
            gosub to.row
            vert = @true
         end else
            * 28 Oct 26 dm (S.88 Phase 4, I3): an area with w{'stay'} keeps the arrow at its first or last row; the form does not move the focus to the next widget
            if not(w{'stay'}) then ret = 'unhandled' : @fm : key
         end
      case key = 'down'
         gosub row.of
         if dr < nr then
            rr = dr + 1
            gosub to.row
            vert = @true
         end else
            if not(w{'stay'}) then ret = 'unhandled' : @fm : key
         end
      case key = 'left'
         if cx > 1 then
            cx -= 1
         end else
            if cy > 1 then
               cy -= 1
               cx = len(lines<cy>) + 1
            end
         end
      case key = 'right'
         if cx <= len(lines<cy>) then
            cx += 1
         end else
            if cy < n then
               cy += 1
               cx = 1
            end
         end
      case key = 'ins' and ek
         w{'over'} = (if ov then 0 else 1)       ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): overwrite mode on or off; nothing is changed in the text, so no undo step
      case key = 'ctrl-home' and ek
         * 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): with edkeys the editor's keys: Ctrl-Home is the start of the text, Ctrl-End its end, Ctrl-Left and Ctrl-Right a word back and on
         cy = 1
         cx = 1
      case key = 'ctrl-end' and ek
         cy = n
         cx = len(lines<n>) + 1
      case key = 'ctrl-right' and ek
         ln = lines<cy>
         if cx > len(ln) then
            if cy < n then
               cy += 1
               cx = 1
            end
         end else
            loop
            while cx <= len(ln) and ln[cx, 1] = ' '
               cx += 1
            repeat
            loop
            while cx <= len(ln) and ln[cx, 1] # ' '
               cx += 1
            repeat
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-left' and ek
         if cx = 1 then
            if cy > 1 then
               cy -= 1
               cx = len(lines<cy>) + 1
            end
         end else
            ln = lines<cy>
            cx -= 1
            loop
            while cx > 1 and ln[cx, 1] = ' '
               cx -= 1
            repeat
            loop
            while cx > 1 and ln[cx - 1, 1] # ' '
               cx -= 1
            repeat
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-a'
         * 28 Oct 26 dm (the owner's D31, 20 Oct): Ctrl-A selects all, as in every editor he named; Home is the start of the row
         my = 1 ; mx = 1
         cy = n
         cx = len(lines<n>) + 1
         want = cx
      case key = 'home'
         gosub row.of
         rr = dr
         gosub row.text
         cx = rstart
      case key = 'end' or key = 'ctrl-e'
         gosub row.of
         rr = dr
         gosub row.text
         cx = rstart + rcm - 1
      case key = 'ctrl-u'                       ;* 14 Oct 26: a word on (was ctrl-f; SED's ctrl-f is find forward)
         ln = lines<cy>
         if cx > len(ln) then
            if cy < n then
               cy += 1
               cx = 1
            end
         end else
            loop
            while cx <= len(ln) and ln[cx, 1] = ' '
               cx += 1
            repeat
            loop
            while cx <= len(ln) and ln[cx, 1] # ' '
               cx += 1
            repeat
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-p'                       ;* 14 Oct 26: a word back (was ctrl-b; SED's ctrl-b is find backward)
         if cx = 1 then
            if cy > 1 then
               cy -= 1
               cx = len(lines<cy>) + 1
            end
         end else
            ln = lines<cy>
            cx -= 1
            loop
            while cx > 1 and ln[cx, 1] = ' '
               cx -= 1
            repeat
            loop
            while cx > 1 and ln[cx - 1, 1] # ' '
               cx -= 1
            repeat
         end
      case key = 'pgup'
         * the view goes back a page (less one row, so the last row of the new page is the first of the old) and the
         * cursor keeps its row on the screen
         gosub row.of
         top -= mh - 1
         if top < 1 then top = 1
         rr = dr - (mh - 1)
         if rr < 1 then rr = 1
         gosub to.row
         vert = @true
      case key = 'pgdn'
         gosub row.of
         top += mh - 1
         if top > nr - mh + 1 then top = nr - mh + 1
         if top < 1 then top = 1
         rr = dr + (mh - 1)
         if rr > nr then rr = nr
         gosub to.row
         vert = @true
      case key = 'ctrl-g'
         if my > 0 then
            my = 0 ; mx = 0
         end else
            my = cy ; mx = cx
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-c'                       ;* 28 Oct 26 (D31): Ctrl-O is no longer a copy alias; it opens a record in the programs that have one
         if has.sel then
            gosub sel.text
            clip.text = st
            clip.has = 1
            my = 0 ; mx = 0
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-t' or key = 'ctrl-f'     ;* ctrl-f is SED's find forward (14 Oct 26); ctrl-t stays
         fdir = 1
         gosub find.prompt
      case key = 'ctrl-b'                       ;* SED's find backward (14 Oct 26)
         fdir = -1
         gosub find.prompt
      case key = 'f3'                           ;* 28 Oct 26 (D31): find next is F3 (and Return in the find question); Ctrl-N is New in the programs that have one
         gosub find.next
      case key = 'shift-f3'                     ;* 28 Oct 26 (Opus's review): and Shift-F3 the one before
         gosub find.prev
      case key = 'alt-m' or ((key = 'ctrl-up' or key = 'ctrl-down') and not(wrap))
         gosub sed.move                  ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 2): swap the cursor and the other end of the selection; nudge the view a line (they change no text, so a viewer has them)
      case ro
         ret = 'unhandled' : @fm : key
      case key = 'tab' and ti > 0
         gosub indent.in                ;* 28 Oct 26: an area with tabindent (the IDE); without it Tab is 'unhandled' below, as ever
      case key = 'btab' and ti > 0
         gosub indent.out
      case key = 'enter'
         kind = 'enter'
         if has.sel then k0 = ''
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then gosub del.sel
         ln = lines<cy>
         lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1]
         rest = ln[cx, len(ln)]
         nb = 0
         if ai then
            * 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): the new line begins with the blanks the old one began with, as many as stand before the cursor
            loop
            while nb < cx - 1 and ln[nb + 1, 1] = ' '
               nb += 1
            repeat
            rest = space(nb) : rest
         end
         ins rest before lines<cy + 1>
         cy += 1
         cx = nb + 1
         want = cx
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case key = 'bs'
         kind = 'bs'
         if has.sel then k0 = ''
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then
            gosub del.sel
         end else
            ln = lines<cy>
            if cx > 1 then
               lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 2] : ln[cx, len(ln)]
               cx -= 1
            end else
               if cy > 1 then
                  prev = lines<cy - 1>
                  lines<cy - 1> = prev : ln
                  del lines<cy>
                  cy -= 1
                  cx = len(prev) + 1
               end
            end
         end
         want = cx
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case key = 'ctrl-d'
         * 28 Oct 26 (D31): Ctrl-D duplicates the line (micro and Geany); it joined or deleted before, which is Del
         kind = 'dup'
         gosub snap.push
         ins lines<cy> before lines<cy + 1>
         cy += 1
         want = cx
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case key = 'del'
         kind = 'del'
         if has.sel then k0 = ''
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then
            gosub del.sel
         end else
            ln = lines<cy>
            if cx <= len(ln) then
               lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : ln[cx + 1, len(ln)]
            end else
               if cy < n then
                  lines<cy> = ln : lines<cy + 1>
                  del lines<cy + 1>
               end
            end
         end
         want = cx
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case key = 'ctrl-del' and ek
         * 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): with edkeys Ctrl-Del deletes the word at the cursor (the blanks before it and the word), or the selection, or at the end of a line joins the next line
         kind = 'delword'
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then
            gosub del.sel
         end else
            ln = lines<cy>
            if cx <= len(ln) then
               ce = cx
               loop
               while ce <= len(ln) and ln[ce, 1] = ' '
                  ce += 1
               repeat
               loop
               while ce <= len(ln) and ln[ce, 1] # ' '
                  ce += 1
               repeat
               lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : ln[ce, len(ln)]
            end else
               if cy < n then
                  lines<cy> = ln : lines<cy + 1>
                  del lines<cy + 1>
               end
            end
         end
         want = cx
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case key = 'ctrl-k' or key = 'alt-k'
         * 28 Oct 26 (D31): Ctrl-K cuts the whole line into the clipboard (micro and Geany); it cut to the end of the row before.  A paste at the start of a line puts it back above it.
         * 8 Nov 26 (S.179): and Alt-K, for a program that keeps Ctrl-K for itself (passkeys: the IDE, where Ctrl-K is the keys list as on every page)
         kind = 'cutline'
         gosub snap.push
         clip.text = lines<cy> : @fm
         clip.has = 1
         if n > 1 then
            del lines<cy>
            if cy > n - 1 then cy = n - 1
         end else
            lines<cy> = ''
         end
         cx = 1
         want = 1
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case key = 'ctrl-x'
         if has.sel then
            kind = 'cut'
            gosub snap.push
            gosub sel.text
            clip.text = st
            clip.has = 1
            gosub del.sel
         end
      case key = 'ctrl-v'
         if clip.has = 1 then
            kind = 'paste'
            gosub snap.push
            if has.sel then gosub del.sel
            gosub paste
            my = 0 ; mx = 0
         end
      case key = 'paste'
         * 28 Oct 26 (S.88 phase 1): the terminal's bracketed paste, one step of undo; $sl_key made the text safe (printable ASCII, line ends as field marks)
         call $sl_mouse('paste.get', pt)
         if pt # '' then
            kind = 'paste'
            gosub snap.push
            if has.sel then gosub del.sel
            t = pt
            gosub paste.t
            my = 0 ; mx = 0
         end
      case key = 'alt-bs' or key = 'alt-d' or key = 'alt-e' or key = 'alt-t' or key = 'alt-u' or key = 'alt-l' or key = 'alt-c' or key = 'alt- ' or key = 'alt-a'
         * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 2): SED's editing commands, in the area itself so that every program with a memo has them (Alt and the letter SED gives
         *   them; the one exception is Alt-E, kill to the end of the line, since Alt-K cuts the whole line here)
         gosub sed.edit
      case key = 'ctrl-z'
         gosub undo.step
      case key = 'ctrl-y'
         gosub redo.step
      case len(key) = 1
         kind = 'type'
         if has.sel then
            kind = 'replace'             ;* a step of its own: what is typed after it is another
         end
         gosub snap.push
         if has.sel then gosub del.sel
         ln = lines<cy>
         if ov and kind = 'type' and cx <= len(ln) then
            lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : key : ln[cx + 1, len(ln)]       ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): overwrite mode (not over a selection, which was deleted and is typed in as before)
         end else
            lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : key : ln[cx, len(ln)]
         end
         cx += 1
         want = cx
         my = 0 ; mx = 0
      case 1
         ret = 'unhandled' : @fm : key
   end case
   w{'lastop'} = kind
   * 18 Oct 26: a key the area does not use (a function key, a Ctrl key the program keeps for itself, up on the first row) changed nothing, so
   *   nothing is drawn again; the whole area was drawn again for it (the IDE's F6 wrote the editor twice)
   if ret<1> = 'unhandled' then return
   gosub count
   if not(vert) and ret<1> = 'handled' then
      gosub row.of
      want = dcx
   end
   gosub scroll
   gosub store
   gosub paint
   return

* 28 Oct 26: tabindent.  in.a, in.b = the lines a selection covers (has.sel from sel.range): a selection that ends at the start of a line leaves that line out
indent.lines:
   in.a = sy
   in.b = ey
   if ex = 1 and ey > sy then in.b = ey - 1
   return

* Tab: blanks to the next stop at the cursor, or the width's blanks before each selected line that holds something (an empty line is left
* empty).  The cursor and the mark move with their line's text; one at the start of a line stays there, so the selection still begins there.
indent.in:
   kind = 'indent'
   gosub snap.push
   if has.sel then
      gosub indent.lines
      for in.i = in.a to in.b
         if lines<in.i> # '' then
            lines<in.i> = space(ti) : lines<in.i>
            if in.i = cy and cx > 1 then cx += ti
            if in.i = my and mx > 1 then mx += ti
         end
      next in.i
   end else
      in.n = ti - mod(cx - 1, ti)
      ln = lines<cy>
      lines<cy> = ln[1, cx - 1] : space(in.n) : ln[cx, len(ln)]
      cx += in.n
   end
   want = cx
   return

* in.ln (a line) -> in.r, the blanks Shift-Tab takes from its start: back to the stop before, at most the width, never more than are there
outdent.count:
   in.l = 0
   loop
   while in.l < len(in.ln) and in.ln[in.l + 1, 1] = ' '
      in.l += 1
   repeat
   in.r = mod(in.l, ti)
   if in.r = 0 then in.r = ti
   if in.r > in.l then in.r = in.l
   return

* Shift-Tab: the blanks before the cursor's line, or before each selected line, back to the stop before.  Nothing to take out: no change and no undo step.
indent.out:
   if has.sel then
      gosub indent.lines
   end else
      in.a = cy
      in.b = cy
   end
   in.any = @false
   for in.i = in.a to in.b
      in.ln = lines<in.i>
      gosub outdent.count
      if in.r > 0 then in.any = @true
   next in.i
   if not(in.any) then return
   kind = 'outdent'
   gosub snap.push
   for in.i = in.a to in.b
      in.ln = lines<in.i>
      gosub outdent.count
      if in.r > 0 then
         lines<in.i> = in.ln[in.r + 1, len(in.ln)]
         if in.i = cy then
            cx -= in.r
            if cx < 1 then cx = 1
         end
         if in.i = my and has.sel then
            mx -= in.r
            if mx < 1 then mx = 1
         end
      end
   next in.i
   want = cx
   return

paint:
   call $sl_attr('text', tc)
   call $sl_attr('scroll', wc)                  ;* 14 Oct 26: wc is only the scroll bar's column; was 'window'
   call $sl_attr('highlight', hc)
   call $sl_attr('gutter', gc)                  ;* 14 Oct 26: was 'disabled', whose background is the window's, not the text's
   call $sl_attr('hotkey', kc)                  ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the style of a breakpoint's mark
   call $sl_scrollbar(mh, top, nr, mh, bar)
   gosub sel.range
   * 19 Oct 26: softcur (1) draws the character under the cursor in the highlight style while the area has the focus, so that the place in the
   * text shows on a terminal whose own cursor does not (the owner could not see it in the IDE, nor which pane had the focus).  Opt in.
   sc.row = -1
   sc.col = 0                                    ;* (both sides of an AND are evaluated: sc.col must have a value when sc.row is tested)
   if w{'softcur'} + 0 and w{'focused'} and not(ro) then
      gosub row.of
      sc.row = dr - top
      sc.col = dcx - left + 1
   end
   sl.line = 0                                   ;* the line whose syntax styles are in sty (rows of a wrapped line share them)
   if syl then gosub syn.sync
   gosub paint.plan
   gosub paint.rows
   if pr.again then                               ;* the edit changed how the next line starts (a comment opened or closed): the rows below it are not as drawn
      pr.all = @true
      pr.again = @false
      sl.line = 0
      gosub paint.rows
   end
   if hb then
      * 18 Oct 26: the horizontal bar in the row under the text: the gutter blank, the bar as wide as the text, a blank under the vertical bar
      gosub get.maxl
      call $sl_hscrollbar(tw, left, maxl, tw, hrow)
      if gut then call $sl_put(scr, w{'ax'}, w{'ay'} + mh, space(gut), str(gc, gut))
      call $sl_put(scr, w{'ax'} + gut, w{'ay'} + mh, hrow : ' ', str(wc, tw + 1))
   end
   if w{'focused'} then
      gosub row.of
      call $sl_curat(w{'ax'} + gut + dcx - left, w{'ay'} + dr - top)
   end
   return

* Which rows this paint has to draw (9 Nov 26, S.184 stage 7; SED draws only the rows it has marked).  pr.kind comes from macro.key, for a key that cannot change anything but the
*   cursor's line ('edit') or the cursor ('move'); every other paint is pr.all.  It holds only if the area is laid out exactly as the last paint left it (psig), with no selection and
*   no wrapping.  An edit also needs the state the line began and ended in from when it was last drawn: pcyl says for which line those are.
paint.plan:
   pr.all = @true
   pr.a = -1
   pr.b = -1
   pr.again = @false
   pr.sig = top : @vm : left : @vm : nr : @vm : gut : @vm : tw : @vm : mh : @vm : w{'ax'} : @vm : w{'ay'} : @vm : ro : @vm : (w{'focused'} + 0) : @vm : has.sel : @vm : wrap : @vm : syl
   if pr.kind # '' and not(has.sel) and not(wrap) and pr.sig = w{'psig'} then
      begin case
         case pr.kind = 'edit' and cy = m.cy
            if not(syl) or w{'pcyl'} + 0 = cy then
               pr.all = @false
               pr.a = cy - top
               if syl then
                  w{'syntl'} = cy                 ;* syn.row starts this line from the state kept for it, not from a checkpoint
                  w{'synts'} = w{'pcys'} + 0
               end
            end
         case pr.kind = 'move'
            pr.all = @false
            if sc.row >= 0 then
               pr.a = sc.row
               pr.b = w{'psc'} + 0
            end
      end case
   end
   w{'psig'} = pr.sig
   w{'psc'} = sc.row
   return

paint.rows:
   for r = 0 to mh - 1
      if not(pr.all) and r # pr.a and r # pr.b then continue
      rr = top + r
      seg = ''
      base = 1
      rline = 0
      if rr <= nr then
         gosub row.text
         seg = convert(char(9), ' ', rtext)
         if not(wrap) then
            base = left
            seg = seg[left, tw]
         end
      end
      seg := space(tw - len(seg))
      attrs = str(tc, tw)
      if syl and rr <= nr then
         gosub syn.row
         if rline = cy then
            if not(pr.all) and pr.kind = 'edit' and sst2 # w{'pcye'} + 0 then pr.again = @true
            w{'pcyl'} = cy
            w{'pcys'} = sst
            w{'pcye'} = sst2
         end
         for k = 1 to len(seg)
            if k > tw then exit
            sc = sty[rstart + base + k - 2, 1]
            if sc # '' and sc # 'n' then attrs[k, 1] = sc
         next k
      end
      if has.sel and rr <= nr then
         for k = 1 to len(rtext) - base + 1
            if k > tw then exit
            ac = rstart + base + k - 2           ;* the character's place in its line
            inside = @true
            if rline < sy or (rline = sy and ac < sx) then inside = @false
            if rline > ey or (rline = ey and ac >= ex) then inside = @false
            if inside then attrs[k, 1] = hc
         next k
      end
      if r = sc.row and sc.col >= 1 and sc.col <= tw then attrs[sc.col, 1] = hc
      if gut then
         * The line numbers: on the first row of a line (a wrapped line's other rows, and the rows past the text, have none)
         gnum = space(gut)
         gsty = str(gc, gut)
         if gn then
            if rr <= nr and rstart = 1 then gnum[bpon + 1, gn] = fmt(rline, (gn - 1) : 'R') : ' '
         end
         if bpon and rr <= nr and rstart = 1 then
            * 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the mark cell, in front of the line numbers, on the first row of a marked line
            locate rline in bpl<1> setting bp.hit then
               gnum[1, 1] = '*'
               gsty[1, 1] = kc
            end
         end
         call $sl_put(scr, w{'ax'} + 0, w{'ay'} + r, gnum, gsty)
      end
      call $sl_put(scr, w{'ax'} + gut, w{'ay'} + r, seg : bar[r + 1, 1], attrs : wc)
   next r
   return

* The syntax styles of the line row rr belongs to (rline): in sty, one style code to each character.  A line's styles are made
* from the state the line before ends in, so the state at the first line shown is found from a checkpoint kept every 100 lines (9 Nov 26,
* S.184 stage 7, as SED does: every line above it, on every paint, was about 100,000 calls per key at line 100,000) and a line that follows
* the last one done goes on from where that finished.
syn.row:
   if rline = sl.line then return
   if sl.line > 0 and rline = sl.line + 1 then
      sst = sst2
   end else
      if rline = w{'syntl'} + 0 then
         sst = w{'synts'} + 0                       ;* the first row shown, as last time: nothing above it has changed (syn.sync)
         sst2 = sst
      end else
         syc.k = int((rline - 1) / 100) + 1
         if syc.k > syc.n then syc.k = syc.n
         sst = if syc.k = 1 then 0 else syc.chk<syc.k> + 0
         sst2 = sst
         for q = ((syc.k - 1) * 100) + 1 to rline - 1
            call $sl_syntax('state', syl, lines<q>, sst, sst2, '')
            sst = sst2
            if mod(q, 100) = 0 then
               syc.k2 = (q / 100) + 1
               if syc.k2 > syc.n then
                  syc.chk<syc.k2> = sst
                  syc.n = syc.k2
               end
            end
         next q
         w{'synchk'} = syc.chk
         w{'synn'} = syc.n
         w{'syntl'} = rline
         w{'synts'} = sst
      end
   end
   call $sl_syntax('line', syl, lines<rline>, sst, sst2, sty)
   sl.line = rline
   return

* The checkpoints as they stand for this text.  A checkpoint depends only on the lines above it, so after an edit those from the first changed line on go.  An edit by a key that
*   works at the cursor (w{'synkey'}, macro.key) is at the cursor's line or the line it was on, and that is taken on trust: the proof, comparing the lines above, costs a copy of the
*   text on every key.  Any other change (the program's, an undo, a paste) is proved or drops them all, and the next paint finds them again.
syn.sync:
   syc.chk = w{'synchk'}
   syc.n = w{'synn'} + 0
   if syc.n < 1 or w{'synfor'} # syl then
      syc.chk = ''
      syc.n = 1
      w{'synfor'} = syl
      w{'syntext'} = lines
      w{'syncy'} = cy
      w{'synchk'} = syc.chk
      w{'synn'} = syc.n
      w{'syntl'} = 0
      return
   end
   syc.old = w{'syntext'}
   if syc.old = lines then                       ;* a text that is one number equal to another as a number has no checkpoints to lose
      w{'syncy'} = cy
      w{'synkey'} = 0
      return
   end
   syc.y = cy
   if w{'syncy'} + 0 >= 1 and w{'syncy'} + 0 < syc.y then syc.y = w{'syncy'} + 0
   syc.same = (syc.y = 1 or w{'synkey'} + 0)
   if not(syc.same) then
      syc.o = index(lines, @fm, syc.y - 1)
      if syc.o then syc.same = ((syc.old[1, syc.o] : '|') = (lines[1, syc.o] : '|'))
   end
   if syc.same then
      syc.k = int((syc.y - 1) / 100) + 1
      if syc.n > syc.k then syc.n = syc.k
      if syc.y < w{'syntl'} + 0 then w{'syntl'} = 0
   end else
      syc.n = 1
      w{'syntl'} = 0
   end
   w{'syntext'} = lines
   w{'syncy'} = cy
   w{'synn'} = syc.n
   w{'synkey'} = 0
   return
end

* END-CODE
